---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import MarketingChrome from "../../components/MarketingChrome.astro";

const phoneDisplay = "(501) 413-3386";
const tel = "tel:+15014133386";
const sms = "sms:+15014133386";
---

<Base
  title='Only Have Instagram for Your Business? Read This | NV Labs'
  description="Instagram is great. A simple website helps you show up on Google and reach people who aren't on IG. Free demo first. Call or text (501) 413-3386."
  image="/og.png"
  robots="index, follow"
>
  <MarketingChrome>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Instagram-only businesses</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Only on Instagram? Here's what a simple website adds</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Keep Instagram for what's new. Add a simple site for Google, hours, and people who aren't on
        IG. Free demo first.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-accent btn-lg" href={tel}>Call {phoneDisplay}</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href={sms}>Text me</a>
        <a class="btn btn-text" href="/free-website-demo/">How the free demo works</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="ig-title">
      <h2 id="ig-title">First off, your Instagram is working</h2>
      <p>
        If you've built a following on Instagram, that's real work and it shows. Lots of local
        businesses get most of their customers from it. I'm not here to tell you to stop.
      </p>
      <p>
        I just want to show you a few things a basic website can do that Instagram can't, so you can
        decide for yourself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="google-title">
      <h2 id="google-title">Google doesn't really show your posts</h2>
      <p>
        When someone searches "taco truck near me" or "nail salon in Henderson," Google mostly shows
        websites and map listings. Your Instagram posts rarely come up in those results.
      </p>
      <p>
        A simple site gives Google something to show. It also backs up your Google Business Profile,
        which is what powers those map results.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="one-title">
      <h2 id="one-title">Everything in one spot</h2>
      <p>
        On Instagram, your hours might be in a highlight, your menu in a pinned post, and your
        address in a caption from last March. Regulars know where to look. New people don't.
      </p>
      <p>A website puts it all on one page:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Hours</li>
        <li>Menu or services</li>
        <li>Address and map</li>
        <li>Phone number</li>
        <li>How to order or book</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="notig-title">
      <h2 id="notig-title">Not everyone is on Instagram</h2>
      <p>
        Some of your future customers don't have an account, or don't want one. Others hit a login
        wall when they tap your link. Think older customers, busy parents, office managers ordering
        for a team. A website opens for anyone with a phone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="bio-title">
      <h2 id="bio-title">It makes your link in bio better</h2>
      <p>
        You can put your website in your Instagram bio. Instead of a cluttered list of links,
        followers land on a clean page with your menu, hours, and a way to order or book. Instagram
        keeps doing what it does well. Your site handles the details.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="risk-title">
      <h2 id="risk-title">Try it with no risk</h2>
      <p>
        I'll build a free demo of your site using your Instagram photos (with your OK) and info. You
        look at it. If you like it, I put it live. If you decide Instagram is enough, that's totally
        fine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
      <h2 id="faq-title">FAQ</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <div>
          <h3>Will a website replace my Instagram?</h3>
          <p>
            No. They work together. Instagram is where people see what's new. Your website is where
            they find the basics and where Google sends people searching for you.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Can you use the photos I already posted?</h3>
          <p>
            Yes, if you're okay with it. Your Instagram photos are often the best ones you have, and
            they keep the site feeling like you.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>I don't have time to keep two things updated. Is that a problem?</h3>
          <p>
            Your site doesn't need daily posts. Once your hours, menu, and contact info are set, it
            mostly stays the same. When something changes, text me.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
      <h2 id="cta-title">See what your Instagram looks like as a website</h2>
      <p>I'll build the demo from what you already have.</p>
      <a class="phone-big" href={tel}>{phoneDisplay}</a>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-accent btn-lg" href={tel}>Call</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href={sms}>Text</a>
      </div>
      <a class="back-home" href="/">Back to NV Labs home</a>
    </section>
  </MarketingChrome>
</Base>
